course: dictionary
lesson: 3440
title: Eyewitness accounts
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An eyewitness is a person who saw something happen with their own eyes, like an accident or a crime. Look at the parts of the word: eye and witness. A witness is someone who sees an event, and an eyewitness was really there.
explain: «Pliny the Younger wrote an eyewitness account of the AD 79 eruption.» Clue words like «wrote» and «account» tell you that this is a report. The word eyewitness tells you that Pliny saw the eruption himself, so his report comes from first-hand experience.
explain: When you meet a new word, ask: could a person have seen this happen? If yes, their words are an eyewitness account. Swap in your meaning, such as «a report by someone who was there», and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Eyewitness accounts are very useful, but people can remember things wrongly. Memory can change over time, so historians and police compare an eyewitness account with other evidence.
fact: An eyewitness is a person who saw something happen with their own eyes.
fact: An eyewitness account is a report written or said by someone who was there.
fact: «Pliny the Younger wrote an eyewitness account of the AD 79 eruption.»
fact: Eyewitness accounts are primary sources, which means they come from people who were present at the event.
fact: Eyewitnesses can remember wrongly, so their accounts are checked against other evidence.
exercise: What is an eyewitness? | A person who writes news stories in an office | A person who was never near the place | *A person who saw something happen with their own eyes | A person who reads about events in books
exercise: Which of these is an eyewitness account? | A list of the best football teams of the year | A map of a town with its roads and parks | A story about a dragon that lived in a cave | *A report written by a girl who saw the bus crash
exercise: «Pliny the Younger wrote an eyewitness account of the AD 79 eruption.» Which word tells you Pliny saw the eruption himself? | account | *eyewitness | eruption | wrote
test: Why do historians study eyewitness accounts? | *Because they are written by people who were there | Because they are always 100% correct, so they never need checking | Because they are the same as fairy tales | Because they are written after everyone has forgotten the event
test: Why might an eyewitness remember something wrongly? | Because they wrote the account in a different language | Because they were looking at the wrong place on purpose | *Because memory can change, even for people who really saw it | Because eyes always lie in the dark
exam: Which sentence uses eyewitness correctly? | The eyewitness account was a recipe for soup | The eyewitness was a bird that flew over the lake | The eyewitness was a cook who baked bread in the village | *The eyewitness told the police that the van drove through the red light
